Add a sbi_platform_final_exit hook that writes a "PARKED__" magic at
a known DRAM offset before the warmboot loop spins, plus a stub
sbi_system_reset_device so SBI SRST is reported supported and falls
through to sbi_exit instead of the kernel halting locally.

Phase 1+2 of bhx#166. Phase 1 was: smallest possible verification that
the SBI SRST fall-through path (sbi_system_reset → stub.system_reset
returns → sbi_exit → sbi_platform_final_exit → sbi_hsm_exit →
jump_warmboot → sbi_hsm_hart_wait) lands in our hook before the harts
park. Phase 2 adds multi-hart convergence: the SRST-issuing hart polls
peer HSM state until every peer has reached SBI_HSM_STATE_STOPPED, then
writes the convergence bitmask + PARKED magic. The host therefore sees
PARKED only when every hart in the tile is fully quiesced, not just
the SRST-issuing one.

Why a stub reset device is needed:

  - Without ANY system_reset_device registered, sbi_system_reset_supported()
    returns false, so the guest kernel's reboot/halt path sees the SBI SRST
    extension as unsupported and just halts in place — never invoking
    SBI SRST, so our final_exit hook never fires.
  - With our stub returning priority 1 from system_reset_check, the kernel
    sees the extension as supported and issues sbi_ecall(SRST). Our stub's
    system_reset() returns immediately (no-op), letting sbi_system_reset
    fall through to sbi_exit().
  - Coexistence with the existing #94 syscon-poweroff path: bhx's daemon
    DTB injects a /poweroff -> syscon node by default. OpenSBI's
    fdt_reset_syscon driver registers a reset device for it (priority 1)
    in the cold-init phase. Our stub registers in final_init (after the
    fdt scan). On a tie at priority 1, the first-registered device wins
    in sbi_system_reset_get_device, so syscon takes precedence by default
    — preserving the existing BRISC-kick path.
  - When the daemon is run with BHX_SOFT_REBOOT=1, modify_dtb skips the
    syscon-poweroff node entirely. With no syscon device registered, our
    stub is the sole reset device → SBI SRST falls through to sbi_exit
    → final_exit → magic written → harts park.

State quiesce: sbi_exit's earlier calls to sbi_timer_exit / sbi_ipi_exit
/ sbi_irqchip_exit handle pending CLINT MSIP / mtimecmp / external IRQs
on the current hart, so we don't duplicate that here. Cache flush + PMP
re-validation are deferred to the Phase 4 release path (see #166).

Status block layout (extends in later phases):
  +0x00 : status word
          0x0000_0000_0000_0000 = not yet parked (cold-boot zero-init)
          0x5f5f_4445_4b52_4150 = "PARKED__" — final_exit reached
  +0x08 : peers convergence bitmask
          Low N bits: hart indices that reached STOPPED before
          the SRST-issuing hart announced PARKED. The SRST-issuing
          hart's bit is NOT set (it transitions itself in sbi_hsm_exit).
          Full convergence on a 4-hart tile: bitmask = 0xE / 0xD /
          0xB / 0x7 depending on which hart issued the SRST.

The status offset 0xE0000 lives in the firmware's reserved range past
OpenSBI's .text/.data/.bss + scratch (typically <300 KiB) and before
the kernel-DTB at +0x100000.

